course: geography
lesson: 48
title: Igneous rocks and the rock cycle
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Igneous rocks are "fire rocks": they form when molten rock cools and hardens, either deep underground or on the surface after an eruption.
explain: How fast the rock cools decides how big its crystals are: slow cooling deep down gives big crystals, quick cooling in the air gives tiny ones, and very quick cooling gives glass with none.
explain: Think of a granite kitchen worktop with its speckles of grey, pink and black; each speckle is a crystal that grew slowly for thousands of years kilometres underground.
explain: All rocks belong to three families, igneous, sedimentary and metamorphic, and over millions of years each can be turned into the others in the rock cycle.
explain: The Sun drives the outside of the cycle with weather, rain and rivers that wear rock down, and the Earth's inner heat drives the inside by melting and squeezing rock deep down.
fact: Igneous rocks form when molten rock cools and hardens.
fact: Rock that cools slowly underground has big crystals, while lava that cools fast on the surface has tiny crystals.
fact: Granite is a speckled igneous rock with big crystals of quartz, feldspar and mica, formed deep underground.
fact: Granite is seen at the surface only after the rocks above it have been worn away, as on Dartmoor in England.
fact: Basalt is a dark, fine-grained igneous rock made from runny lava and is the most common rock of the ocean floor.
fact: Obsidian is natural glass that forms when lava cools so fast that no crystals can grow.
fact: Pumice is a volcanic rock so full of gas bubbles that it can float on water.
fact: All rocks belong to three families: igneous, sedimentary and metamorphic.
fact: Rocks are slowly recycled from one type into another in the rock cycle.
fact: In the rock cycle, rocks are worn down into bits that make sedimentary rock, changed by heat and pressure into metamorphic rock, or melted and cooled into igneous rock.
fact: The rock cycle is powered by the Sun's energy at the surface and the Earth's inner heat below.
fact: One trip around the rock cycle usually takes millions of years.
exercise: How do igneous rocks form? | When molten rock cools and hardens | When sand is pressed together | When shells pile up on the sea floor | When rock is squeezed without melting
exercise: Why does granite have big crystals? | It cooled slowly deep underground | It cooled fast in the open air | It was made from sand grains | It was melted again by the Sun's heat
exercise: Why does lava on the surface make rock with tiny crystals? | It cools fast, so crystals have no time to grow | It is squeezed hard by the weight of the air above | It is made of mud, not melted rock | It cools very slowly over thousands of years
exercise: What minerals make the speckles in granite? | Quartz, feldspar and mica | Salt, sugar and chalk | Iron, gold and silver | Clay, sand and shells
exercise: What is basalt? | A dark, fine-grained igneous rock from runny lava | A white rock made of tiny shells | A soft, crumbly rock made of mud from rivers | A speckled rock with big crystals of quartz and mica
exercise: What is the most common rock of the ocean floor? | Basalt | Granite | White marble | Soft chalk
exercise: What is obsidian? | Natural glass made when lava cools too fast for crystals | A rock made of seashells | A rock with very big crystals that cooled slowly underground | A soft rock full of holes that floats on water
exercise: Why can pumice float on water? | It is full of gas bubbles | It is made of light, dry wood | It is hollow inside like a ball | It is lighter than air itself
exercise: What are the three families of rocks? | Igneous, sedimentary and metamorphic | Hard, soft and medium | Granite, basalt and marble | Old, young and middle-aged rocks
exercise: What is the rock cycle? | Rocks slowly turning from one type into another | Rocks rolling down hills in a big circle | Rocks being cut up in quarries | The yearly melting of all the rocks in the world
exercise: What powers the rock cycle? | The Sun's energy at the surface and the Earth's inner heat below | The Moon's pull on the rocks | Wind alone, blowing across the land and wearing it down | Plants growing their roots deep into rocks and cracking them into bits
exercise: How long does one trip around the rock cycle usually take? | Millions of years | About one year, like the seasons | About a hundred years | About a week or two
exercise: What happens to igneous rock worn into sand and pressed into layers? | It becomes sedimentary rock | It becomes lava again at once | It slowly turns into metal ore | It disappears from the Earth
exercise: Why can you see granite on Dartmoor if it forms deep underground? | The rocks above it have been worn away | It was carried up there by rivers | It grew upwards out of the ground like a tree | It was dropped there by a meteorite
exercise: Which part of the rock cycle does the Sun drive? | Weathering and erosion by weather, rain and rivers | Melting rock deep underground into hot magma | Squeezing rocks into marble | Making magma in the mantle
test: Ella's granite worktop has big speckles. Her brother says it cooled fast in a volcano's lava. Put him right. | Big crystals mean it cooled slowly deep underground | He's right, fast cooling in lava always makes big crystals | Granite has no crystals at all | Granite is made of glued-together sand
test: "found a rock that floats in the bath lol, is it fake?" | It's probably pumice, real rock full of gas bubbles | It must be plastic, real rocks can never float | It's obsidian, the glassy rock that floats | It's granite with lots of air trapped inside it
test: In a fantasy film, a black glassy stone is called "dragonglass". What is it in real life? | Obsidian, lava that cooled too fast for crystals | Basalt, the rock of the ocean floor | Coal pressed into glass | Granite polished very smooth
test: What would happen to the rock cycle if the Earth's inside went completely cold? | No melting or squeezing deep down, so new igneous and metamorphic rock would stop | Rocks would start melting faster | The Sun would take over and melt all the rocks from above instead of from below | Nothing at all would change
test: Mia says: "A rock is a rock for ever, it never changes." What's true? | Over millions of years rocks are worn, squeezed or melted into other rocks | She's right, rocks never change at all | Rocks change into other kinds of rock every few weeks, after heavy rain | Only igneous rocks can change
test: Two pieces of rock from the same volcano: one has tiny crystals, one is glassy. Why the difference? | The glassy one cooled even faster | The glassy one cooled slowly underground | They came from different planets | The glassy one was made of sand
test: "is sedimentary rock basically just rocks made by volcanoes?" | No, volcanoes make igneous rock; sedimentary is pressed-together bits | Yes, all rocks come from volcanoes in the end | Yes, but only the dark ones | No, sedimentary rocks are made by plants growing and dying on top of them
test: Your little brother thinks the ocean floor is made of sand all the way down. What lies under the mud and sand? | Mostly basalt from runny lava | Mostly granite with big crystals | Mostly chalk made of shells | Mostly ice
test: Why can't you watch one full trip round the rock cycle in your lifetime? | It usually takes millions of years | It only happens at night | It happens far too deep for anyone to ever see | It stopped long ago
test: A pebble of basalt is ground into sand by waves. Which family could that sand become part of next? | Sedimentary rock | Igneous rock straight away | Metal ore | No rock ever again
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the formation of igneous rocks? | They are formed by the cooling and solidification of molten rock | They are formed by the cementation of compacted sediment | They are formed by the alteration of rock by heat and pressure without melting | They are formed by the accumulation of the shells of marine organisms
exam: Identify the principal factor determining the dimensions of the crystals within an igneous rock. | The rate at which the molten rock cooled | The colour of the molten rock prior to cooling | The quantity of sunlight received by the rock | The age of the surrounding sedimentary strata
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the sources of energy which drive the rock cycle. | The energy of the Sun at the surface and the internal heat of the Earth | The gravitational attraction of the Moon exclusively | The kinetic energy of the wind, acting exclusively upon the surface | The chemical energy released by plants in the soil
exam: It is given that a specimen of volcanic rock floats upon water. Ascertain the most probable identity of the specimen. | Pumice | Granite | Dense basalt | Obsidian
